## Proof

We prove that for every integer $r \ge 1$, there are infinitely many primes $p$ with $p \equiv 1 \pmod{2^r}$.

**Key tool: cyclotomic polynomial.** Recall that $\Phi_{2^r}(x) = x^{2^{r-1}} + 1$.

**Lemma.** Let $q$ be an odd prime and $a$ an integer with $\gcd(a,q)=1$. If $q \mid \Phi_{2^r}(a) = a^{2^{r-1}}+1$, then $\operatorname{ord}_q(a) = 2^r$, and consequently $2^r \mid q-1$, i.e., $q \equiv 1 \pmod{2^r}$.

*Proof of Lemma.* From $q \mid a^{2^{r-1}}+1$ we get $a^{2^{r-1}} \equiv -1 \pmod{q}$. Squaring gives $a^{2^r} \equiv 1 \pmod{q}$, so $\operatorname{ord}_q(a) \mid 2^r$. Since $q$ is odd, $-1 \not\equiv 1 \pmod{q}$, so $a^{2^{r-1}} \not\equiv 1 \pmod{q}$, meaning $\operatorname{ord}_q(a) \nmid 2^{r-1}$. The only divisor of $2^r$ that does not divide $2^{r-1}$ is $2^r$ itself, so $\operatorname{ord}_q(a) = 2^r$. By Fermat's little theorem, $\operatorname{ord}_q(a) \mid q-1$, hence $2^r \mid q-1$. $\square$

**Main argument (Euclid-style).** Suppose, for contradiction, that there are only finitely many primes $\equiv 1 \pmod{2^r}$; call them $p_1, p_2, \ldots, p_k$ (the list may be empty, in which case the product below is $1$). Set

$$P = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_k, \qquad A = 2P, \qquad N = \Phi_{2^r}(A) = A^{2^{r-1}} + 1.$$

Since $A \ge 2$, we have $N \ge 2^{2^{r-1}} + 1 \ge 3 > 1$, so $N$ has at least one prime factor $q$. Since $A$ is even, $A^{2^{r-1}}$ is even, so $N$ is odd; hence $q$ is odd.

We check $\gcd(A, q) = 1$: if $q \mid A$, then $q \mid A^{2^{r-1}}$, and combined with $q \mid A^{2^{r-1}}+1$ this gives $q \mid 1$, a contradiction. So $\gcd(A,q) = 1$.

By the Lemma, $q \equiv 1 \pmod{2^r}$.

It remains to show $q$ is **new**, i.e., $q \notin \{p_1, \ldots, p_k\}$. For each $i$, since $p_i \mid P \mid A$, we have $A \equiv 0 \pmod{p_i}$, so

$$N = A^{2^{r-1}} + 1 \equiv 0 + 1 = 1 \pmod{p_i}.$$

Thus $p_i \nmid N$, so $q \neq p_i$ for every $i$.

We have produced a prime $q \equiv 1 \pmod{2^r}$ not in the list $\{p_1, \ldots, p_k\}$, contradicting the assumption that the list was complete. Therefore there are infinitely many primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod{2^r}$. $\blacksquare$

